tel 1.0

base dissonance
import soundness

info   minimal edit scripts between sequences, as values to apply, invert, align, serialize and redraft
detail
    _Dissonance_ computes the difference between two sequences as an immutable value. `diff`
    runs Myers' algorithm over any two `Sequence`s, with equality or a comparison of the
    caller's choosing, and returns a `Diff`: an edit script of insertions (`Ins`), deletions
    (`Del`) and unchanged elements (`Par`), each with its positions in the two sequences.

    A `Diff` can be applied to the original as a patch, inverted with `flip`, grouped into
    changed and unchanged runs, refined with `rdiff` into an `RDiff` which pairs deletions with
    the insertions that replaced them as substitutions (`Sub`), serialized in the unix `diff`
    format and read back from it. A `Redraft` is the forgiving form of a patch, which names only
    the lines to remove and add and finds where they belong, and an `Evolution` follows each
    element through many versions rather than two.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val original = List(t"foo", t"bar", t"baz")
    val changes = diff(Sequence(t"foo", t"bar", t"baz"), Sequence(t"foo", t"quux", t"baz"))

    changes.patch(original)                      // t"foo", t"quux", t"baz"
    changes.serialize.read[Diff[Text]].patch(original)
    ```

    A diff computed by `diff`, or resolved from a redraft, carries every element in its edits,
    and says so in its type: it is a `Diff[element] & Retained`, on which `kept` reads an edit's
    value without handling absence. A diff read from the unix format has no values for its
    unchanged lines, so it is a plain `Diff`, and cannot be rendered as a redraft.

    The library is the single source set `dissonance.core`, which compiles for the JVM, Scala.js
    and Scala Native. Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella.

refer dissonance.diff
refer dissonance.Diff
refer dissonance.RDiff
refer dissonance.Redraft

entry .diff   the minimal edit script turning one sequence into another, by Myers' algorithm
    `diff(left, right)` compares two `Sequence`s and returns the fewest insertions and
    deletions which turn `left` into `right`, interleaved with the unchanged elements, as a
    `Diff`. A third argument replaces `==` with any comparison, such as a case-insensitive one;
    an element of `right` paired with an equal one of `left` is reported as a `Par` holding the
    element from `left`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    diff(Sequence('A', 'C'), Sequence('A', 'B', 'C'))
    // Diff(Par(0, 0, 'A'), Ins(1, 'B'), Par(1, 2, 'C'))

    diff(Sequence(t"Foo", t"bar"), Sequence(t"foo", t"bar"), _.lower == _.lower).size  // 0
    ```

    Every edit the algorithm makes holds its element, so the result is a `Diff[element] &
    Retained`: it can be rendered as a `Redraft`, and `kept` reads its edits' values directly.
    The elements may be of any type.

entry .Diff   an edit script: the insertions, deletions and unchanged elements between two sequences
  detail
      A `Diff` is a sequence of `#edits`, each an `Ins`, a `Del` or a `Par`, in the order they
      apply. Each carries its position in the left sequence, the right sequence, or both, and,
      optionally, its element. It is usually made by `diff`, but may be built by hand or read
      from the unix format.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val changes = diff(Sequence(t"A", t"B"), Sequence(t"B", t"C", t"D"))
      // Diff(Del(0, t"A"), Par(1, 0, t"B"), Ins(1, t"C"), Ins(2, t"D"))

      changes.size                           // 3
      changes.patch(List(t"A", t"B"))         // t"B", t"C", t"D"
      changes.flip.patch(List(t"B", t"C", t"D"))
      ```

      A diff of `Text` lines serializes to the unix `diff` format with `.serialize`, and that
      format is read back with `read[Diff[Text]]`, through `.aggregable`. A retained diff of
      `Text` renders as a `Redraft` with `.redraft`.
  refer dissonance.diff
  refer dissonance.RDiff
  refer dissonance.Redraft
  entry .aggregable   reads a `Diff[Text]` from the lines of a unix-format diff, as `read[Diff[Text]]`
      The given which makes `Diff[Text]` readable from a stream of `Text` lines in the default
      (`diff` with no options) format: command lines such as `2c2,3`, `3d2` and `0a1`, deleted
      lines prefixed `< `, inserted lines prefixed `> `, and `---` between them. It needs a
      `Tactic[Diff.Error]`, which is raised for a line it cannot read.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Chain(t"2c2,3", t"< bar", t"---", t"> quux", t"> bop").read[Diff[Text]]
      // Diff(Par(0, 0), Del(1, t"bar"), Ins(1, t"quux"), Ins(2, t"bop"))
      ```

      The format does not repeat the unchanged lines, so the `Par`s of a parsed diff carry no
      values: it patches the original perfectly well, but is a plain `Diff`, not a `Retained`
      one, and so cannot be rendered with `.redraft`.
  entry .serialize   a diff of text as the lines of a unix-format diff
      An extension on `Diff[Text]`, returning a `Chain[Text]`: for each changed region, a
      command line (`<left>a<right>`, `<left>d<right>` or `<left>c<right>`, with ranges
      written `start,end`), then each deleted line prefixed `< `, a `---` line if the region
      both deletes and inserts, and each inserted line prefixed `> `. Unchanged lines are not
      written. The lines read back with `read[Diff[Text]]`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      diff(Sequence(t"a", t"b", t"c", t"d"), Sequence(t"a", t"d")).serialize
      // t"2,3d1", t"< b", t"< c"
      ```
  entry .redraft   renders a retained diff of text as a `Redraft`, with as much context as asked for
      An extension on `Diff[Text] & Retained`, such as the result of `diff`. The context is a
      `Redraft.Context`: `Minimal` (the default) keeps only the unchanged lines needed to place
      each change unambiguously, `Full` keeps them all, and `Fixed(n)` keeps those within `n`
      lines of a change. A redraft rendered from a diff always applies to the original to give
      the diff's target.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Minimal context: only what is needed to place the change
        code
            val source = Sequence(t"a", t"b", t"c", t"d", t"e")
            val target = Sequence(t"a", t"b", t"C", t"d", t"e")

            diff(source, target).redraft().serialize
            // t"- c", t"+ C"
      frame
        label Fixed context: one line either side of the change
        code
            val source = Sequence(t"a", t"b", t"c", t"d", t"e")
            val target = Sequence(t"a", t"b", t"C", t"d", t"e")

            diff(source, target).redraft(Redraft.Context.Fixed(1)).serialize
            // t"b", t"- c", t"+ C", t"d"
      frame
        label Full context: every line of the original
        code
            val source = Sequence(t"a", t"b", t"c", t"d", t"e")
            val target = Sequence(t"a", t"b", t"C", t"d", t"e")

            diff(source, target).redraft(Redraft.Context.Full).serialize
            // t"a", t"b", t"- c", t"+ C", t"d", t"e"
      ##
      ```

      A parsed diff, whose unchanged lines have no values, is not `Retained` and so has no
      `redraft`: the type rules out a rendering which could not reproduce the original.
  entry .Error   raised when a line of a unix-format diff cannot be read
    detail
        Error 39: _could not read the diff at line `lineNo`: `line`_. Raised by
        `Diff.aggregable` (`read[Diff[Text]]`) for a command line whose ranges are not numbers,
        or which is not a command at all. A malformed range aborts the read; an unrecognised
        command line is raised and then skipped, so under a tactic which recovers, the rest of
        the diff is still read.
    entry #lineNo   the line number, counting from 1, of the line which could not be read
    entry #line     the text of the line which could not be read
  entry #edits     the edits, in the order they apply
  entry #size      the number of insertions and deletions, not counting unchanged elements
      Zero exactly when the two sequences were equal.
  entry #flip      the inverse diff, which turns the right sequence back into the left
      Every `Ins` becomes a `Del` and every `Del` an `Ins`, and the positions of each `Par` are
      swapped. The element type becomes `Optional[element]`, since an inverted insertion is a
      deletion whose value is present but typed as optional.
  entry #map       applies a function to the element of every edit
      Positions are kept; an edit with no value stays without one. The result is a plain
      `Diff`, not a `Retained` one: re-mark it with `retained` if every edit still carries its
      value.
  entry #patch     applies the diff to the original sequence, giving the target
    detail
        `diff.patch(original)` walks the edits over a `List` of the original elements:
        insertions are taken from the diff, deletions skip an element, and unchanged elements
        are kept. It returns a `Chain`. For a `Par` with a value, an optional second argument
        `update(fromDiff, fromOriginal)` chooses what is emitted; by default it is the diff's
        own value, and for a `Par` with no value, as in a parsed diff, it is the original's.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val changes = diff(Sequence(t"foo", t"bar", t"baz"), Sequence(t"foo", t"quux", t"baz"))
        changes.patch(List(t"foo", t"bar", t"baz"))   // t"foo", t"quux", t"baz"
        ```

        A diff which deletes or keeps beyond the end of the sequence it is applied to panics:
        it was computed against a different original.
  entry #rdiff     pairs each deletion with a similar insertion as a substitution, giving an `RDiff`
    detail
        `diff.rdiff(similar)` looks at each changed region of the diff (each `Tract.Changed`)
        and, where it both deletes and inserts, pairs deletions with insertions as `Sub`s.
        Within a region, the pairing is itself a diff of the deleted elements against the
        inserted ones, with `similar` as the comparison, so as many similar pairs as possible
        are found, in order; those left over stay as plain `Del`s and `Ins`s.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import proximities.levenshteinProximity
        import caseSensitivity.caseSensitive

        val italian = Sequence(t"zero", t"uno", t"due", t"tre")
        val spanish = Sequence(t"cero", t"uno", t"dos", t"tres")

        diff(italian, spanish).rdiff(_.proximity(_) < 4)
        // RDiff(Sub(0, 0, t"zero", t"cero"), Par(1, 1, t"uno"),
        //       Sub(2, 2, t"due", t"dos"), Sub(3, 3, t"tre", t"tres"))
        ```

        The second parameter, `subSize` (default `1`), pairs a region outright, without
        consulting `similar`, when it has equal numbers of deletions and insertions and no
        more than `subSize` of each: at that size the correspondence is obvious even when the
        values have nothing in common. An edit with no value, as in a parsed diff, is never
        similar to anything.
  entry #collate   the edits grouped into alternating runs of unchanged and changed elements
      A `List` of `Tract`s: each maximal run of `Par`s is a `Tract.Unchanged`, and each run of
      deletions and insertions between them a `Tract.Changed`, holding its deletions and its
      insertions separately.
  entry #chunks    the changed regions, each with its positions and its deletions and insertions
      A `Chain` of `Chunk`s, one for each changed region, as the unix format writes them; the
      unchanged elements between them are not represented. `Diff.serialize` is written over
      this.

entry .Edit   one step of a `Diff`: an insertion, a deletion or an unchanged element
  detail
      The sealed supertype of `Ins`, `Del` and `Par`, the three edits a `Diff` is made of. An
      `Edit` is also a `Change`, the wider type of an `RDiff`'s steps, which adds `Sub`.

      Every edit has a `#value`, which is `Optional`: an `Ins` always has one, but a `Del` or a
      `Par` may not, as in a diff read from the unix format. On an edit whose type is
      `Retained`, `kept` reads the value as a bare element.
  refer dissonance.Change
  refer dissonance.Retained
  entry #value   the element the edit concerns, if it is carried
  entry #map     applies a function to the edit's element, keeping its positions
      The result is an `Edit` of the same kind; the `Retained` mark is not kept, and may be
      re-established with `retained`.

entry .Change   one step of an `RDiff`: an edit, or a substitution of one element for another
  detail
      The sealed supertype of `Edit` (so of `Ins`, `Del` and `Par`) and of `Sub`, the
      substitution which `Diff#rdiff` makes from a similar deletion and insertion. A match over
      an `RDiff`'s `#changes` has four cases.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def describe(change: Change[Text]): Text = change match
        case Par(_, _, _)       => t"keep"
        case Del(_, _)          => t"remove"
        case Ins(_, value)      => t"add $value"
        case Sub(_, _, _, _)    => t"change"
      ```
  refer dissonance.Edit
  refer dissonance.RDiff
  entry #map   applies a function to the element, or elements, of the change, keeping its positions

entry .Ins   an insertion: an element of the right sequence which is not in the left
  detail
      `Ins(right, value)`: `value` is inserted at position `right` of the right sequence. An
      insertion always carries its value, since a patch could not otherwise produce it.
  entry #right   the inserted element's position in the right sequence, from zero
  entry #value   the inserted element

entry .Del   a deletion: an element of the left sequence which is not in the right
  detail
      `Del(left, value)`: the element at position `left` of the left sequence is removed. The
      value is `Optional` and defaults to `Unset`, since a patch needs only the position; a
      diff from `diff` always records it, and one read from the unix format does too.
  entry #left    the deleted element's position in the left sequence, from zero
  entry #value   the deleted element, if it is carried

entry .Par   an unchanged element, present at a position in each sequence
  detail
      `Par(left, right, value)`: the element at position `left` of the left sequence is the
      one at `right` of the right. The value defaults to `Unset`: a diff read from the unix
      format, which does not repeat unchanged lines, has none.
  entry #left    the element's position in the left sequence, from zero
  entry #right   the element's position in the right sequence, from zero
  entry #value   the element, if it is carried

entry .Sub   a substitution: an element of the left sequence replaced by a similar one in the right
  detail
      Made only by `Diff#rdiff`, from a deletion and an insertion it judged to correspond.
      `Sub(left, right, leftValue, rightValue)` says that the element at `left`, `leftValue`,
      became the element at `right`, `rightValue`. Both values are `Optional`, as a parsed
      diff's deletions may have none. `RDiff#flip` swaps the two sides.
  entry #left         the replaced element's position in the left sequence
  entry #right        the replacing element's position in the right sequence
  entry #leftValue    the element which was replaced, if it is carried
  entry #rightValue   the element which replaced it, if it is carried

entry .RDiff   an aligned diff, in which a similar deletion and insertion are paired as a `Sub`
  detail
      The result of `Diff#rdiff`: the same steps as a `Diff`, except that where a deletion
      and an insertion correspond, they are one `Sub`. It suits display — a changed line shown
      as a modification of the old one, rather than as a removal and an unrelated addition —
      and the comparison of structured records, where a modified record is the same record.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val aligned = diff(Sequence(t"a", t"b"), Sequence(t"a", t"c")).rdiff(_ == _)
      // RDiff(Par(0, 0, t"a"), Sub(1, 1, t"b", t"c"))
      ```
  refer dissonance.Diff
  refer dissonance.Change
  entry #changes   the steps, in order: `Par`, `Del`, `Ins` and `Sub`
  entry #flip      the inverse aligned diff, from the right sequence back to the left
      Insertions and deletions exchange places, and each `Par` and `Sub` has its two positions
      (and, for a `Sub`, its two values) swapped.
  entry #map       applies a function to the element, or elements, of every step

entry .Chunk   one changed region of a diff: where it is, what it deletes and what it inserts
  detail
      The unit of the unix `diff` format, given by `Diff#chunks`: the region's starting
      positions in the left and right sequences, then its deletions and its insertions.
  entry #position        the region's starting position in the left sequence
  entry #rightPosition   the region's starting position in the right sequence
  entry #dels            the region's deletions, in order
  entry #inss            the region's insertions, in order

entry .Tract   a run of a diff: either a changed region or a stretch of unchanged elements
  detail
      `Diff#collate` divides a diff into alternating `Tract`s. A `Changed` tract holds a
      region's deletions and insertions separately, which is what `Diff#rdiff` pairs; an
      `Unchanged` one holds the `Par`s between regions.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      diff(Sequence(1, 2, 3), Sequence(1, 4, 3)).collate.map:
        case Tract.Unchanged(pars)      => t"${pars.size} kept"
        case Tract.Changed(dels, inss)  => t"${dels.size} for ${inss.size}"
      ```
  entry .Changed   a changed region: its deletions and its insertions
    entry #deletions    the region's deletions, in order
    entry #insertions   the region's insertions, in order
  entry .Unchanged   a run of unchanged elements
    entry #retentions   the `Par`s of the run, in order

entry .Retained   the type-level proof that every edit of a diff carries its element
    `Retained` is a refinement, `Any { type Retain = true }`, which marks an `Edit` or a
    `Diff` whose values are known to be present. `diff` and `Redraft#resolve` return a
    `Diff[element] & Retained`; a diff read from the unix format, or built by hand, does
    not. Being a refinement rather than a trait, the mark costs nothing at runtime: an
    `Edit[Text] & Retained` is an `Edit` like any other.

    Two operations need the proof. `kept` reads an edit's value as a bare element instead of
    an `Optional`, and `Diff.redraft` renders a diff as a `Redraft`, which must reproduce every
    kept and deleted line.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val changes: Diff[Char] & Retained = diff(Sequence('a', 'b'), Sequence('b'))
    changes.retentions.map(_.kept)   // 'a', 'b', each a Char
    ```

    `retained` marks a value as retained, for a diff or edit known by construction to carry
    its values, and `retentions` gives a retained diff's edits with the mark.

entry .retained   marks an edit or a diff as `Retained`, where every value is known to be present
    An extension on `Edit` and on `Diff`, returning the receiver typed `& Retained`. It checks
    nothing: it is the place where code which has just supplied every value records the fact,
    as `diff` and `Redraft#resolve` do. Marking a diff whose edits lack values makes `kept`
    return absent values as if they were elements, and a rendered redraft wrong.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val changes = Diff(Par(0, 0, t"a"), Ins(1, t"b")).retained
    changes.redraft().serialize   // t"a", t"+ b": the context places the insertion
    ```

    `Edit#map` and `Diff#map` drop the mark, which must be re-applied with `retained` when the
    mapped values are still all present.

entry .kept   an edit's value: a bare element on a `Retained` edit, and `Optional` otherwise
    An extension on any `Edit`. On an edit whose static type is `Retained`, it is a
    `transparent inline` accessor which returns the element itself, so no absence need be
    handled; on any other edit, or where the type is not known, it is `#value`, an
    `Optional[element]`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val edit = Del(0, t"gone").retained
    val text: Text = edit.kept
    ```

entry .retentions   a retained diff's edits, each typed as `Retained`
    An extension on `Diff[element] & Retained`, giving its `#edits` as a `List[Edit[element] &
    Retained]`, so that `kept` can be applied to each to read a bare element.

entry .Redraft   a forgiving patch of text lines: only the changes, located by searching the original
  detail
      The unix `diff` format states each change's position exactly, and does not apply to a
      text which has moved. A `Redraft` states only the lines to remove and the lines to add,
      with as few unchanged lines as are needed to place them, and finds where they belong by
      searching the original. It is the form in which a person, or a language model, can
      propose an edit to a text it has only read.

      A redraft is a sequence of `Redraft.Directive`s, usually parsed from lines of text with
      `Redraft.parse`:

      - `- line` removes `line`, and `+ line` inserts `line`;
      - `< line` and `> line` remove and insert unconditionally;
      - any other line is context, which must match a line of the original;
      - a line beginning `\` is context with the `\` removed, for a context line which itself
        begins with a marker.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val source = Sequence(t"line1", t"line2", t"line3")

      Redraft.parse(Chain(t"- line2", t"+ new line 2a")).patch(source)
      // t"line1", t"new line 2a", t"line3"
      ```

      Context and removed lines are matched in order, each against the first matching line
      after the one before, so unchanged lines between them need not be written; an insertion
      goes after the last line matched. A `- x` or `+ x` line is ambiguous when the original
      also has the literal line `- x` or `+ x`, which it might be context for: the ambiguity is
      reported, and the unconditional `<`/`>` spellings resolve it. A change which would also
      match further down — `- a` against `a`, `b`, `a` — is under-anchored and reported, rather
      than applied at a guess; one more context line anchors it.

      `#patch` applies a redraft, `#resolve` turns it into the `Diff` it describes, and
      `#verify` lists its problems without raising. `Diff.redraft` renders a diff as a redraft,
      and `#serialize` writes one back as lines.
  refer dissonance.Diff
  refer dissonance.Redraft.Error
  entry .parse   reads a redraft from lines of text
      Each line becomes one `Redraft.Directive`: `+ ` and `- ` (or a bare `+` or `-`) a `Mark`
      inserting or removing the rest of the line, `> ` and `< ` an unconditional `Add` or
      `Cut`, a line beginning `\` a `Keep` of the rest, and any other line a `Keep` of itself.
      Parsing never fails; whether the redraft applies is checked against an original by
      `Redraft#resolve`, `Redraft#patch` or `Redraft#verify`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Redraft.parse(Chain(t"< forced", t"> add", t"\\- escaped"))
      // Redraft(Cut(t"forced"), Add(t"add"), Keep(t"- escaped"))
      ```
  entry .render   renders a retained diff of text as a redraft with the given context
      The implementation of `Diff.redraft`, which is the usual way to call it. `Minimal`
      context is found greedily: context lines are dropped, furthest from a change first, for
      as long as the redraft still applies to the original without ambiguity and gives the
      same result. An insertion or removal which would be ambiguous as `+` or `-` is written as
      `>` or `<`.
  entry .Directive   one line of a redraft: context, an insertion, a removal or an ambiguous mark
    detail
        What `Redraft.parse` makes of each line, and what `Redraft#serialize` writes back.
        `Mark` is the ordinary `+`/`-` form, which is read as an insertion or removal unless
        the original has the literal marked line as context; `Add` and `Cut` are the
        unconditional `>`/`<` forms.
    entry .Keep   a context line, which must match a line of the original
      entry #line   the line's text
    entry .Add    an unconditional insertion, written `> line`
      entry #line   the line to insert
    entry .Cut    an unconditional removal, written `< line`, of a line which must be in the original
      entry #line   the line to remove
    entry .Mark   an insertion or removal, written `+ line` or `- line`
      entry #line     the line to insert or remove, without its marker
      entry #insert   `true` for an insertion (`+`), `false` for a removal (`-`)
  entry .Anomaly   a problem found in applying a redraft: where, what, and why
      Returned by `Redraft#verify`, and the substance of a `Redraft.Error`. `line` is the
      index, from zero, of the directive at fault, `text` the line it named, and `reason` a
      `Redraft.Error.Reason`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Redraft.parse(Chain(t"- a")).verify(Sequence(t"a", t"b", t"a"))
      // List(Redraft.Anomaly(0, t"a", Redraft.Error.Reason.Unanchored))
      ```
  entry .Context   how much unchanged context a rendered redraft keeps
    detail
        Passed to `Diff.redraft`. `Minimal` keeps only the context lines without which the
        redraft would be ambiguous or would apply elsewhere; `Full` keeps every unchanged
        line; `Fixed(lines)` keeps those within `lines` of a change, as a unified diff does.
    entry .Fixed   the unchanged lines within a fixed distance of a change
      entry #lines   how many lines of context to keep either side of each change
  entry .Error   raised when a redraft cannot be applied to the original
    detail
        Error 472: _the redraft could not be applied at line `line` (`text`) because `reason`_.
        Raised by `Redraft#resolve` and `Redraft#patch`, for the first anomaly found; use
        `Redraft#verify` to list them all.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val source = Sequence(t"line1", t"line2")
        capture[Redraft.Error](Redraft.parse(Chain(t"absent", t"- line2")).patch(source))
        . reason   // Redraft.Error.Reason.NoMatch
        ```
    entry .Reason   why a directive of a redraft could not be applied
      detail
          `NoMatch` (_no matching line could be found in the original_): a context or removed
          line is not in the original after the lines matched before it. `Ambiguous` (_the
          change could be interpreted in more than one way_): a `+`/`-` line could be either a
          change or a context line for the literal marked line; write it with `>`/`<`, or
          escape the context with `\`. `Unanchored` (_there is not enough context to locate the
          change unambiguously_): the line would also match further on; add a context line.
      entry .communicable   describes each reason as a message
      entry #number   the reason's number, the second part of the error's code
    entry #line     the index, from zero, of the directive which could not be applied
    entry #text     the line of text the directive named
    entry #reason   why it could not be applied, as a `Redraft.Error.Reason`
  entry #directives   the redraft's directives, in order
  entry #serialize    the redraft as lines of text, which `Redraft.parse` reads back
      `Mark`s are written `+ ` and `- `, `Add` and `Cut` as `> ` and `< `, and a context line
      which begins with a marker or a `\` is escaped with a `\`.
  entry #resolve      the `Diff` the redraft describes against an original, or a `Redraft.Error`
      Matches the redraft's directives against `original`, with `==` or the given comparison,
      and returns a `Diff[Text] & Retained` over every line of the original. It raises a
      `Redraft.Error` for the first anomaly, so needs a `Tactic[Redraft.Error]`.
  entry #verify       every anomaly the redraft has against an original, without raising
      An empty `List` means the redraft applies cleanly.
  entry #patch        applies the redraft to an original, giving the edited lines
      `#resolve` followed by `Diff#patch`: a `Chain[Text]`, or a `Redraft.Error`, raised
      through the `Tactic[Redraft.Error]` in scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Redraft.parse(Chain(t"b", t"- a")).patch(Sequence(t"a", t"b", t"a"))   // t"a", t"b"
      ```

entry .evolve   tracks elements through a series of versions, identifying each across them all
    `evolve(versions)` diffs each version against the next and merges the results into one
    `Evolution`: a single sequence of atoms, each an element with the set of versions it is
    present in. An element which disappears and later returns is recognized as the same atom
    where the diffs allow it. The optional `similar` comparison makes each step an aligned
    diff (`Diff#rdiff`), so that a modified element continues its atom rather than starting a
    new one. The element type needs a `ClassTag`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val versions = List(List('d', 'o', 'g'), List('c', 'a', 't'), List('d', 'o', 'g'))
    val evolution = evolve(versions)

    evolution(Ter)   // List('d', 'o', 'g'): the third version
    ```

entry .Evolution   a history of many versions, as one sequence of elements tagged with their versions
  detail
      Made by `evolve`. Its `#sequence` interleaves every element of every version, in an
      order consistent with each, and each `Evolution.Atom` records the versions, by
      `Ordinal`, in which it appears. Applied to an ordinal, it reconstructs that version
      exactly, which is the structure a document history, an undo stack or a series of drafts
      wants.
  refer dissonance.evolve
  entry .Atom   one element of an evolution, with the versions it is present in
    entry #value      the element
    entry #presence   the ordinals of the versions in which the element appears
    entry #add        the same atom, present also in another version
    entry #has        whether the element is present in a version
  entry #sequence   every element of every version, in order, each with its versions
  entry #apply      reconstructs one version, by its ordinal (`Prim` for the first)
